WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:06.214
A browser lets a page read a response from another site only when that site agrees.

00:00:06.214 --> 00:00:11.838
The CORS Configuration feature of Orchard Core lets you decide which sites may call yours.

00:00:11.838 --> 00:00:15.880
Let's enable it, create a policy, and keep its settings narrow.

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:23.220
Every page a browser loads has an origin:

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:25.260
a scheme, a host, and a port.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:29.573
When a page from one origin calls another origin, such as your site,

00:00:29.573 --> 00:00:31.380
the call is cross-origin.

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:36.113
The browser looks for CORS headers in the responses of your site.

00:00:36.113 --> 00:00:40.713
They tell it whether the page's origin, method, and headers are allowed, and

00:00:40.713 --> 00:00:43.125
it blocks the page when they are not.

00:00:43.125 --> 00:00:45.930
A site with no policy sends no CORS headers.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:48.770
CORS is enforced by browsers.

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:53.190
It does not sign anyone in, it does not check permissions, and tools

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:55.459
that are not browsers ignore it.

00:00:55.459 --> 00:00:59.640
Your APIs still need their own authentication and authorization.

00:01:03.914 --> 00:01:07.276
Open Tools, Features, and search for CORS.

00:01:07.276 --> 00:01:13.301
The CORS Configuration feature is in the Security category, and it says, Enables

00:01:13.301 --> 00:01:15.894
configuration of CORS settings.

00:01:16.354 --> 00:01:17.681
Select Enable.

00:01:17.681 --> 00:01:22.524
A message confirms that the feature CORS Configuration was enabled.

00:01:22.977 --> 00:01:26.819
Then open Settings, Security, Cross-Origin Resource Sharing.

00:01:26.819 --> 00:01:33.107
This page requires the Managing Cors Settings permission, which the Administrator role has by default.

00:01:33.577 --> 00:01:38.794
The page warns that the current tenant, which is your site, reloads when you save.

00:01:38.794 --> 00:01:41.581
The list is empty, and says Nothing here!

00:01:41.581 --> 00:01:44.427
There are no CORS policies for the moment.

00:01:48.627 --> 00:01:50.378
Select Add a policy.

00:01:50.378 --> 00:01:55.700
The editor opens with the name New policy, and with sections for Details,

00:01:55.700 --> 00:02:00.147
Credentials, Origins, Headers, Methods, and Exposed headers.

00:02:00.605 --> 00:02:03.844
In Details, set Policy name to Partner portal.

00:02:03.844 --> 00:02:06.336
Then select Set as default policy:

00:02:06.336 --> 00:02:10.945
the default policy is the one that the site applies to its requests.

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:14.622
In Credentials, Allow credentials starts selected.

00:02:14.622 --> 00:02:19.382
It lets the browser send cookies and other sign-in information with the call,

00:02:19.382 --> 00:02:21.218
when the page asks for it.

00:02:21.218 --> 00:02:25.520
A partner that reads only public pages does not need it, so clear it.

00:02:25.973 --> 00:02:28.985
In Origins, leave Allow any origin cleared.

00:02:28.985 --> 00:02:33.656
Under Allowed origins, type the origin of the partner site, which is a

00:02:33.656 --> 00:02:38.082
scheme, a host, and an optional port, with no path and no trailing

00:02:38.082 --> 00:02:40.233
slash, and select Add Origin.

00:02:40.685 --> 00:02:43.213
Headers and Methods work the same way.

00:02:43.213 --> 00:02:47.521
A policy starts by allowing any header and any method, so clear both,

00:02:47.521 --> 00:02:49.933
and add only what the partner needs:

00:02:49.933 --> 00:02:52.805
the Content-Type header, and the GET method.

00:02:53.272 --> 00:02:57.982
Exposed headers lists response headers that scripts on the partner's pages may read,

00:02:57.982 --> 00:03:00.546
beyond the ones that browsers always allow.

00:03:00.546 --> 00:03:03.162
Leave it empty unless the partner needs one.

00:03:03.614 --> 00:03:04.636
Select Save.

00:03:04.636 --> 00:03:09.973
The tenant reloads, a message confirms that the CORS settings have updated successfully,

00:03:09.973 --> 00:03:13.834
and the list shows Partner portal with a Default Policy badge.

00:03:18.068 --> 00:03:24.289
Allow any origin lets every site call yours, and it ignores the list of allowed origins.

00:03:24.289 --> 00:03:30.378
Let's edit Partner portal, and select Allow credentials and Allow any origin together.

00:03:30.842 --> 00:03:31.983
Select Save.

00:03:31.983 --> 00:03:37.436
A warning says that this combination is insecure, and names the affected policy.

00:03:37.436 --> 00:03:42.191
The admin saves nothing when any policy has it, and an asterisk entry

00:03:42.191 --> 00:03:45.552
in the allowed origins is treated the same way.

00:03:49.752 --> 00:03:52.449
Allow only what each client needs:

00:03:52.449 --> 00:03:58.857
its origins, its methods, and its headers, and expose only the response headers it reads.

00:03:58.857 --> 00:04:05.332
Use HTTPS origins in production, and list every trusted origin when you allow credentials.

00:04:06.082 --> 00:04:08.125
Mark one policy as the default.

00:04:08.125 --> 00:04:12.155
The other policies in the list stay registered by name, so a module

00:04:12.155 --> 00:04:15.082
can select one of them for a specific endpoint.

00:04:15.832 --> 00:04:20.993
With the Deployment feature enabled, a deployment plan can include the Cors settings

00:04:20.993 --> 00:04:25.122
step, which exports the policies, ready to import on another site.

00:04:25.872 --> 00:04:29.461
When a browser reports a CORS failure, check four things.

00:04:29.461 --> 00:04:34.018
The call is cross-origin, the feature is enabled on the site that receives

00:04:34.018 --> 00:04:37.892
it, the policy is the default, and the origin matches exactly.

00:04:39.892 --> 00:04:44.911
Allow the origins, methods, and headers that each partner needs, mark one policy

00:04:44.911 --> 00:04:48.354
as the default, and keep authentication on every API.

00:04:48.354 --> 00:04:49.872
Thanks for watching.

